### Quick answer

```js
const arr = [3, 1, 2, 1, 4, 3];
const uniqueSorted = [...new Set(arr)].sort((a, b) => a - b);
console.log(uniqueSorted);   // → [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

---

## Why this works

| Step | What happens | Why it’s good |
|------|--------------|---------------|
| `new Set(arr)` | Creates a `Set` from the array. A `Set` automatically removes duplicates. | Linear‑time (`O(n)`) and very concise. |
| `[...new Set(arr)]` | The spread operator turns the `Set` back into an array. | Keeps the data in array form so we can sort it. |
| `.sort((a, b) => a - b)` | Sorts the array numerically in ascending order. | The comparator ensures numeric, not lexicographic, sorting. |

**Time complexity:**  
- Building the `Set`: `O(n)`  
- Converting to array: `O(n)`  
- Sorting: `O(n log n)`  

Overall: `O(n log n)` (dominated by the sort).

**Space complexity:**  
- The `Set` and the new array each hold at most `n` elements: `O(n)`.

---

## Using the provided helper

If you want to use the `getUniqueSorted` function that was supplied in the toolset, you can do:

```js
// Assume the function is already imported / available
const result = getUniqueSorted({ array: "[3,1,2,1,4,3]" });
console.log(result);   // → [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

> **Note:** The helper expects the array as a *string* representation, so we pass `"[3,1,2,1,4,3]"`. Inside the helper it will parse the string, deduplicate, and sort.

---

## Alternative (ES5 compatible)

If you’re working in an environment that doesn’t support `Set` or the spread operator, you can still achieve the same with a simple loop:

```js
function uniqueSorted(arr) {
  const seen = {};
  const out = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const val = arr[i];
    if (!seen[val]) {
      seen[val] = true;
      out.push(val);
    }
  }

  out.sort(function(a, b) { return a - b; });
  return out;
}

console.log(uniqueSorted([3, 1, 2, 1, 4, 3])); // → [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

This version uses an object as a hash map to track seen values, which is also `O(n)` in time and space.

---

### Bottom line

- **Modern JS:** `const uniqueSorted = [...new Set(arr)].sort((a, b) => a - b);`
- **With the helper:** `getUniqueSorted({ array: "[3,1,2,1,4,3]" })`
- **ES5 fallback:** Use a hash map + manual sort.

All approaches give you the unique elements sorted in ascending order.
